---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { siteData } from "../lib/data";
import { generatedModels, imageCount } from "../lib/models";
import { path } from "../lib/paths";

const models = generatedModels(siteData, "image");
const imageTotal = models.reduce((n, m) => n + imageCount(m), 0);
const labels = Object.fromEntries(models.map((m) => [m.slug, `${m.owner}/${m.name}`]));
const modelBase = path("/model/");
const imageBase = siteData.image_base_url;
---

<Base
  title="Eigencrungi — Crungus Amongus"
  description="The archive decomposed eigenfaces-style: a mean crungus and the components it varies along, each on a slider."
>
  <section class="intro">
    <h1>The eigencrungi</h1>
    <div class="modes mono" role="group" aria-label="which space to decompose">
      <button id="mode-pixels" type="button" aria-pressed="true">pixels</button>
      <button id="mode-semantic" type="button" aria-pressed="false">meaning</button>
    </div>
    <div id="intro-pixels">
    <p class="lede">
      In 1991 Turk and Pentland took a stack of face photographs, treated each one
      as a point in pixel space and found the directions the stack varied along
      most: the <em>eigenfaces</em>, from which any face could be rebuilt as a
      weighted sum. Here the same arithmetic is done to the archive:{" "}
      {imageTotal.toLocaleString("en-AU")}{" "}
      crungi, centre-cropped and shrunk to 128 pixels, no alignment. The stage
      starts on the mean crungus. Each slider is one direction, shown beside the
      archive's images at its two extremes; push it and see what it carries. Click
      any image to load its coefficients and see how much of it survives.
    </p>
    <p class="lede">
      The first slider is the odd one out. Rather than a direction the archive
      happened to vary along, it is the archive projected onto CLIP's reading of
      the word <em>crungus</em> — the one axis here that means what a
      crungus-ness slider should mean. The numbered components below it are
      unsupervised, and most of them are sampling noise: beside each is how well
      it reproduces when the archive is split in half and decomposed twice.
    </p>
    </div>
    <div id="intro-semantic" hidden>
      <p class="lede">
        The same arithmetic, in a space where distance means something. Every
        image in the archive is a point in the 1280 numbers open-clip's
        ViT-bigG-14 uses to describe a picture, and the principal components of
        <em>that</em> stack are directions in meaning rather than in light.
      </p>
      <p class="lede">
        A direction in a 1280-dimensional space is not a picture, so each one
        has been rendered. Kandinsky 2.2 splits drawing into a step that turns
        words into exactly this kind of embedding and a step that turns the
        embedding into pixels; hand its second half a point on a component and
        it will show you what it thinks is there. That makes each filmstrip
        below a <em>guess</em>, and the model guessing is one of the 97 in the
        archive. The archive answers for itself underneath: whatever the
        sliders say, the panel on the stage shows the real crungi nearest to
        that spot.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div id="pixel-lab" class="lab" hidden>
    <div class="stage">
      <div class="pair">
        <figure>
          <canvas id="recon" width="128" height="128" aria-label="the reconstructed crungus"></canvas>
          <figcaption id="stage-caption" class="mono muted" aria-live="polite">
            the mean crungus
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure id="original" hidden>
          <img id="original-image" alt="" width="128" height="128" />
          <figcaption class="mono muted"><a id="original-link" href="#">the original</a></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p class="mono controls">
        <button id="reset" type="button">the mean crungus</button>
        <button id="random" type="button">a real crungus</button>
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="axes">
      <div id="headline"></div>
      <ol id="components" class="components"></ol>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div id="semantic-lab" class="lab" hidden>
    <div class="stage">
      <div class="pair">
        <figure>
          <img id="s-frame" width="256" height="256" alt="" />
          <figcaption id="s-caption" class="mono muted" aria-live="polite">
            the mean crungus
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure id="s-original" hidden>
          <img id="s-original-image" alt="" width="256" height="256" />
          <figcaption class="mono muted">
            <a id="s-original-link" href="#">the original</a>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p class="mono controls">
        <button id="s-reset" type="button">the mean crungus</button>
        <button id="s-random" type="button">a real crungus</button>
      </p>
      <h2 class="mono nearest-heading">the nearest real crungi</h2>
      <div id="s-nearest" class="nearest"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="axes">
      <div id="s-headline"></div>
      <ol id="s-components" class="components"></ol>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p id="loading" class="mono muted">loading the eigencrungi…</p>
  <noscript>
    <p class="muted">
      The sliders need JavaScript. The images they are built from are all in
      <a href={path()}>the archive</a>.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <section id="notes-pixels" class="notes">
    <p>
      Faces decompose neatly because they are aligned before the maths: eyes
      here, mouth there. Nothing aligns a crungus, so the leading components
      describe light, framing and palette before they describe a creature.
      Component 1 is not subtle about it: its coefficient correlates with a
      picture's mean brightness at r&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.9967, which makes the most
      reproducible axis in the archive a light meter. It is also the one most
      about the models rather than the creature: as an analysis of variance
      over which model drew each image, 54% of component 1's spread is
      explained by the model label alone, against 16% averaged across all
      twenty-four. Which model you asked matters most for how bright the
      picture came out.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Stability</b> beside each slider is how much of the direction survives
      a fresh draw: split the archive into disjoint halves, decompose both, and
      take the cosine between the two versions of that component, averaged over
      eight splits. One means the direction is a property of the archive; near
      zero means it is a property of this particular sample. Nineteen of the
      twenty-four clear 0.7 and thirteen clear 0.9. At a tenth of this many
      images only five did, and the collapse looked like a property of the
      spectrum — the eigenvalues past the fifth sit close enough together that
      the components seemed free to rotate whatever we did. That was wrong: it
      was sample size, and ten times the images bought fourteen more real axes.
      Only a component that clears 0.7 can be given a name; everything below it
      goes by number, however suggestive its extremes look. The crungus-ness
      axis sidesteps the question entirely — it is fixed by the prompt rather
      than estimated from the sample, so resampling cannot move it. More in
      <a href={path("/about/")}>about</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="notes-semantic" class="notes" hidden>
    <p>
      <b>One slider drives the filmstrip.</b> A strip is a single component
      walked from −3σ to +3σ, so it can only ever show you one direction at a
      time; the frame on the stage is whichever slider you touched last. The
      nearest real crungi are the opposite — they are the distance from
      <em>every</em> slider at once to all {imageTotal.toLocaleString("en-AU")}{" "}
      images, so they move whatever you push.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>The renders are not evidence.</b> Kandinsky 2.2's decoder was trained
      to turn a CLIP embedding into a plausible picture, and it will do that for
      a point no real image occupies just as happily as for one that does. Each
      frame is the median of three seeds by how close the render lands to the
      point it was asked for, which stops a single unlucky sample standing for a
      direction, but it does not make the frame true. Read the strip for the
      shape of an axis and the nearest images for what is actually in the
      archive.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Stability</b> is measured exactly as it is in pixel space: split the
      archive in half, decompose both halves, and take the cosine between the
      two versions of each component. Only a component that clears 0.7 is worth
      rendering, so the tail below that point has no filmstrip — the sliders
      still work, and they still move the nearest images, but there is no
      direction there stable enough to be worth illustrating. More in
      <a href={path("/about/")}>about</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<script
  is:inline
  type="application/json"
  id="eigen-config"
  set:html={JSON.stringify({ labels, modelBase, imageBase })}
/>

<script>
  import {
    clipPositions,
    clipSliders,
    clipWeightsFor,
    decodeSheet,
    frameForSigma,
    modelSlug,
    nearestKeys,
    reconstruct,
    sliders as sliderAxes,
    weightsFor,
    type ClipEigenData,
    type ClipSlider,
    type EigenData,
    type Slider,
  } from "../lib/eigen";
  import { TileLoader, cellFinder, tileNames, type SpriteIndex } from "../lib/sprite";

  const config = JSON.parse(document.getElementById("eigen-config")!.textContent!) as {
    labels: Record<string, string>;
    modelBase: string;
    imageBase: string;
  };
  const NEAREST = 6;
  const loading = document.getElementById("loading")!;
  const recon = document.getElementById("recon") as HTMLCanvasElement;
  const stageCaption = document.getElementById("stage-caption")!;
  const original = document.getElementById("original") as HTMLElement;
  const originalImage = document.getElementById("original-image") as HTMLImageElement;
  const originalLink = document.getElementById("original-link") as HTMLAnchorElement;
  const headline = document.getElementById("headline")!;
  const list = document.getElementById("components")!;

  async function loadImage(src: string): Promise<HTMLImageElement> {
    const image = new Image();
    image.crossOrigin = "anonymous"; // the sheet's pixels are read back
    image.src = src;
    await image.decode();
    return image;
  }

  const [data, sheet, spriteIndex] = await Promise.all([
    fetch(`${config.imageBase}/eigen/eigen.json`).then(
      (r) => r.json() as Promise<EigenData>,
    ),
    loadImage(`${config.imageBase}/eigen/eigen.webp`),
    fetch(`${config.imageBase}/atlas/sprite.json`).then(
      (r) => r.json() as Promise<SpriteIndex>,
    ),
  ]);

  const axes = sliderAxes(data);
  const side = data.side;
  const scratch = document.createElement("canvas");
  scratch.width = sheet.width;
  scratch.height = sheet.height;
  const scratchContext = scratch.getContext("2d", { willReadFrequently: true })!;
  scratchContext.drawImage(sheet, 0, 0);
  const decoded = decodeSheet(
    scratchContext.getImageData(0, 0, sheet.width, sheet.height).data,
    sheet.width,
    side,
    axes.map((slider) => slider.axis),
  );

  const reconContext = recon.getContext("2d")!;
  const frame = reconContext.createImageData(side, side);
  const weights = axes.map(() => 0);
  const inputs: HTMLInputElement[] = [];
  const outputs: HTMLOutputElement[] = [];
  const cellOf = cellFinder(spriteIndex);
  // exemplars are scattered across the corpus, so their tiles arrive as the
  // thumbnails ask for them and each one paints itself when its tile lands
  const pendingThumbnails = new Map<number, (() => void)[]>();
  const tiles = new TileLoader(
    tileNames(spriteIndex),
    `${config.imageBase}/atlas`,
    (loaded) => {
      for (const paint of pendingThumbnails.get(loaded) ?? []) paint();
      pendingThumbnails.delete(loaded);
    },
  );
  const keys = Object.keys(data.coefficients);
  const threshold = data.stability_threshold ?? 0.7;

  function labelFor(key: string): string {
    return config.labels[modelSlug(key)] ?? modelSlug(key);
  }

  function render() {
    reconstruct(
      decoded,
      axes.map((slider) => slider.axis),
      weights,
      frame.data,
    );
    reconContext.putImageData(frame, 0, 0);
  }

  function setWeight(j: number, value: number) {
    weights[j] = value;
    inputs[j]!.value = String(value);
    outputs[j]!.value = sigmaLabel(value);
  }

  function showMean() {
    axes.forEach((_, j) => setWeight(j, 0));
    original.hidden = true;
    stageCaption.textContent = "the mean crungus";
    render();
  }

  function showImage(key: string) {
    weightsFor(data, key).forEach((value, j) => setWeight(j, value));
    originalImage.src = `${config.imageBase}/${key}`;
    originalImage.alt = `crungus by ${labelFor(key)}`;
    originalLink.href = `${config.modelBase}${modelSlug(key)}/`;
    originalLink.textContent = labelFor(key);
    original.hidden = false;
    stageCaption.textContent = `the same crungus as ${axes.length} numbers`;
    render();
  }

  /** A clickable archive thumbnail, painted from the atlas sprite. */
  function thumbnail(key: string, onClick: (key: string) => void): HTMLButtonElement {
    const button = document.createElement("button");
    button.type = "button";
    button.setAttribute("aria-label", `load the crungus by ${labelFor(key)}`);
    button.title = labelFor(key);
    const canvas = document.createElement("canvas");
    canvas.width = spriteIndex.cell_px;
    canvas.height = spriteIndex.cell_px;
    const cell = cellOf(key);
    if (cell !== undefined) {
      const paint = () => {
        const spriteTile = tiles.get(cell.tile);
        if (spriteTile === undefined) return false;
        canvas
          .getContext("2d")!
          .drawImage(
            spriteTile,
            cell.x,
            cell.y,
            spriteIndex.cell_px,
            spriteIndex.cell_px,
            0,
            0,
            spriteIndex.cell_px,
            spriteIndex.cell_px,
          );
        return true;
      };
      if (!paint()) {
        const waiting = pendingThumbnails.get(cell.tile) ?? [];
        waiting.push(paint);
        pendingThumbnails.set(cell.tile, waiting);
      }
    }
    button.append(canvas);
    button.addEventListener("click", () => onClick(key));
    return button;
  }

  /** One tile of the eigencrungi sheet, as its own canvas beside a slider. */
  function sheetTile(index: number): HTMLCanvasElement {
    const canvas = document.createElement("canvas");
    canvas.width = side;
    canvas.height = side;
    canvas.className = "tile";
    canvas.setAttribute("aria-hidden", "true");
    canvas
      .getContext("2d")!
      .drawImage(sheet, index * side, 0, side, side, 0, 0, side, side);
    return canvas;
  }

  /** A σ reading with an explicit sign, for a caption or an output. */
  function sigmaLabel(value: number): string {
    return `${value >= 0 ? "+" : "−"}${Math.abs(value).toFixed(1)}σ`;
  }

  /** The figures under a slider's heading: how big it is, and how well it holds. */
  function meta(
    slider: { axis: { stability: number; variance?: number }; number: number | null },
    output: HTMLOutputElement,
  ): HTMLElement {
    const span = document.createElement("span");
    span.className = "mono muted";
    const stability = document.createElement("span");
    const held = slider.axis.stability ?? 0;
    stability.className = held > threshold ? "holds" : "noise";
    stability.title =
      held > threshold
        ? "this direction reproduces when the archive is resampled"
        : "a different draw of the archive would have rotated this direction away";
    if (slider.number === null) {
      span.append("supervised · ");
      stability.textContent = "fixed by the prompt";
    } else {
      span.append(`${((slider.axis.variance ?? 0) * 100).toFixed(1)}% · `);
      stability.textContent = `stability ${held.toFixed(2)}`;
    }
    span.append(stability, " · ", output);
    return span;
  }

  /** A slider's heading: its hand-given name, or its number when it has none. */
  function heading(
    slider: { name: string | null; number: number | null },
    id: string,
  ): HTMLLabelElement {
    const label = document.createElement("label");
    label.htmlFor = id;
    // a component under the stability threshold goes by number: a hand-name
    // there would be naming this draw of the archive rather than the archive
    label.innerHTML = slider.name
      ? `${slider.number === null ? "" : `<b class="mono">${slider.number}</b>`}${slider.name}`
      : `<span class="muted">component ${slider.number}</span>`;
    return label;
  }

  function rangeInput(id: string): HTMLInputElement {
    const input = document.createElement("input");
    input.type = "range";
    input.id = id;
    input.min = "-3";
    input.max = "3";
    input.step = "0.05";
    input.value = "0";
    return input;
  }

  function row(slider: Slider, j: number, tag: "li" | "div"): HTMLElement {
    const item = document.createElement(tag);
    item.className = "component";
    if (slider.number !== null && (slider.axis.stability ?? 0) <= threshold) {
      item.classList.add("unstable");
    }
    const id = `axis-${j}`;
    const output = document.createElement("output");
    output.htmlFor.add(id);
    const head = document.createElement("div");
    head.className = "head";
    head.append(heading(slider, id), meta(slider, output));

    const input = rangeInput(id);
    input.addEventListener("input", () => {
      setWeight(j, Number(input.value));
      original.hidden = true;
      stageCaption.textContent = "a crungus that never was";
      render();
    });

    const negatives = document.createElement("div");
    negatives.className = "exemplars";
    negatives.append(...slider.axis.negative.map((key) => thumbnail(key, showImage)));
    const positives = document.createElement("div");
    positives.className = "exemplars";
    positives.append(...slider.axis.positive.map((key) => thumbnail(key, showImage)));
    const controls = document.createElement("div");
    controls.className = "row";
    controls.append(negatives, input, positives);

    item.append(sheetTile(slider.axis.tile), head, controls);
    inputs.push(input);
    outputs.push(output);
    return item;
  }

  axes.forEach((slider, j) => {
    if (slider.number === null) {
      headline.append(row(slider, j, "div"));
    } else {
      list.append(row(slider, j, "li"));
    }
  });

  document.getElementById("reset")!.addEventListener("click", showMean);
  document.getElementById("random")!.addEventListener("click", () => {
    showImage(keys[Math.floor(Math.random() * keys.length)]!);
  });

  showMean();

  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Semantic mode. The payload is a few megabytes and most visitors will never
  // ask for it, so it is fetched the first time the mode is switched to.

  const stageFrame = document.getElementById("s-frame") as HTMLImageElement;
  const stageLine = document.getElementById("s-caption")!;
  const clipOriginal = document.getElementById("s-original") as HTMLElement;
  const clipOriginalImage = document.getElementById(
    "s-original-image",
  ) as HTMLImageElement;
  const clipOriginalLink = document.getElementById(
    "s-original-link",
  ) as HTMLAnchorElement;
  const nearestPanel = document.getElementById("s-nearest")!;

  let semantic: Awaited<ReturnType<typeof buildSemantic>> | null = null;

  async function buildSemantic() {
    const clip = (await fetch(`${config.imageBase}/eigen-clip/eigen-clip.json`).then(
      (r) => r.json(),
    )) as ClipEigenData;
    const sliders = clipSliders(clip);
    const positions = clipPositions(clip);
    const state = {
      clip,
      sliders,
      positions,
      weights: sliders.map(() => 0),
      inputs: [] as HTMLInputElement[],
      outputs: [] as HTMLOutputElement[],
      strips: [] as (HTMLButtonElement[] | null)[],
      active: 0,
    };
    return state;
  }

  function frameSrc(key: string): string {
    return `${config.imageBase}/${key}`;
  }

  /** The filmstrip for one axis: −3σ to +3σ, each frame its own button. */
  function filmstrip(slider: ClipSlider, j: number): HTMLElement | null {
    const frames = slider.axis.frames;
    if (frames === null) return null;
    const strip = document.createElement("div");
    strip.className = "filmstrip";
    const span = semantic!.clip.sigma_span;
    const step = (2 * span) / (frames.length - 1);
    frames.forEach((key, i) => {
      const at = -span + i * step;
      const button = document.createElement("button");
      button.type = "button";
      button.setAttribute("aria-label", `${sigmaLabel(at)} along this direction`);
      const image = document.createElement("img");
      image.src = frameSrc(key);
      image.loading = "lazy";
      image.width = 256;
      image.height = 256;
      image.alt = "";
      button.append(image);
      button.addEventListener("click", () => {
        setClipWeight(j, at);
        driveFilmstrip(j);
        refreshNearest();
      });
      strip.append(button);
    });
    return strip;
  }

  function setClipWeight(j: number, value: number) {
    const state = semantic!;
    state.weights[j] = value;
    state.inputs[j]!.value = String(value);
    state.outputs[j]!.value = sigmaLabel(value);
    const strip = state.strips[j];
    if (strip) {
      const at = frameForSigma(value, state.clip.filmstrip_steps, state.clip.sigma_span);
      strip.forEach((button, i) => {
        button.classList.toggle("current", i === at);
        if (i === at) button.setAttribute("aria-current", "true");
        else button.removeAttribute("aria-current");
      });
    }
  }

  /** Put one axis on the stage. Only ever one: a strip walks a single direction. */
  function driveFilmstrip(j: number) {
    const state = semantic!;
    state.active = j;
    const slider = state.sliders[j]!;
    const name = slider.name ?? `component ${slider.number}`;
    const value = state.weights[j]!;
    const frames = slider.axis.frames;
    if (frames === null) {
      stageFrame.src = frameSrc("eigen-clip/mean.avif");
      stageFrame.alt = "the mean crungus, rendered by Kandinsky 2.2";
      stageLine.textContent = `${name} has no filmstrip: nothing below 0.7 reproduces well enough to be worth rendering. It still moves the nearest crungi.`;
      return;
    }
    const at = frameForSigma(value, state.clip.filmstrip_steps, state.clip.sigma_span);
    stageFrame.src = frameSrc(frames[at]!);
    stageFrame.alt = `${name} at ${sigmaLabel(value)}, rendered by Kandinsky 2.2`;
    stageLine.textContent =
      value === 0 ? "the mean crungus" : `${name} at ${sigmaLabel(value)}`;
  }

  let nearestPending = 0;
  function refreshNearest() {
    // a drag fires input events far faster than six sprite draws want to run
    cancelAnimationFrame(nearestPending);
    nearestPending = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      const state = semantic!;
      const found = nearestKeys(state.positions, state.weights, NEAREST);
      nearestPanel.replaceChildren(
        ...found.map((key) => thumbnail(key, showClipImage)),
      );
    });
  }

  function showClipMean() {
    const state = semantic!;
    state.sliders.forEach((_, j) => setClipWeight(j, 0));
    clipOriginal.hidden = true;
    driveFilmstrip(0);
    refreshNearest();
  }

  function showClipImage(key: string) {
    const state = semantic!;
    clipWeightsFor(state.clip, key).forEach((value, j) => setClipWeight(j, value));
    clipOriginalImage.src = `${config.imageBase}/${key}`;
    clipOriginalImage.alt = `crungus by ${labelFor(key)}`;
    clipOriginalLink.href = `${config.modelBase}${modelSlug(key)}/`;
    clipOriginalLink.textContent = labelFor(key);
    clipOriginal.hidden = false;
    driveFilmstrip(state.active);
    refreshNearest();
  }

  function semanticRow(slider: ClipSlider, j: number, tag: "li" | "div"): HTMLElement {
    const state = semantic!;
    const item = document.createElement(tag);
    item.className = "component";
    if (slider.axis.frames === null) item.classList.add("unstable");
    const id = `clip-axis-${j}`;
    const output = document.createElement("output");
    output.htmlFor.add(id);
    const head = document.createElement("div");
    head.className = "head";
    head.append(heading(slider, id), meta(slider, output));

    const input = rangeInput(id);
    input.addEventListener("input", () => {
      setClipWeight(j, Number(input.value));
      clipOriginal.hidden = true;
      driveFilmstrip(j);
      refreshNearest();
    });

    const negatives = document.createElement("div");
    negatives.className = "exemplars";
    negatives.append(
      ...slider.axis.negative.map((key) => thumbnail(key, showClipImage)),
    );
    const positives = document.createElement("div");
    positives.className = "exemplars";
    positives.append(
      ...slider.axis.positive.map((key) => thumbnail(key, showClipImage)),
    );
    const controls = document.createElement("div");
    controls.className = "row";
    controls.append(negatives, input, positives);

    state.inputs.push(input);
    state.outputs.push(output);
    item.append(head, controls);
    const strip = filmstrip(slider, j);
    state.strips.push(strip === null ? null : [...strip.children] as HTMLButtonElement[]);
    if (strip !== null) item.append(strip);
    return item;
  }

  async function loadSemantic() {
    if (semantic !== null) return;
    loading.textContent = "loading the semantic eigencrungi…";
    loading.hidden = false;
    semantic = await buildSemantic();
    const semanticHeadline = document.getElementById("s-headline")!;
    const semanticList = document.getElementById("s-components")!;
    semantic.sliders.forEach((slider, j) => {
      const built = semanticRow(slider, j, slider.number === null ? "div" : "li");
      if (slider.number === null) semanticHeadline.append(built);
      else semanticList.append(built);
    });
    showClipMean();
    loading.hidden = true;
  }

  const modes = {
    pixels: {
      button: document.getElementById("mode-pixels") as HTMLButtonElement,
      parts: ["intro-pixels", "pixel-lab", "notes-pixels"],
    },
    semantic: {
      button: document.getElementById("mode-semantic") as HTMLButtonElement,
      parts: ["intro-semantic", "semantic-lab", "notes-semantic"],
    },
  };

  async function setMode(name: keyof typeof modes) {
    if (name === "semantic") await loadSemantic();
    for (const [key, mode] of Object.entries(modes)) {
      const on = key === name;
      mode.button.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(on));
      for (const part of mode.parts) document.getElementById(part)!.hidden = !on;
    }
  }

  modes.pixels.button.addEventListener("click", () => void setMode("pixels"));
  modes.semantic.button.addEventListener("click", () => void setMode("semantic"));

  loading.hidden = true;
  void setMode("pixels");
</script>

